.TH KILL 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kill \- send a signal to processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
.RB [ \-s
.I signal
|
.BI \- signal
|
.BR \-\- ]
.IR pid ...
.br
.B kill
.B \-l
.RI [ exit_status | signal ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kill
sends a signal, by default
.BR SIGTERM ,
to each process named by a
.I pid
operand, using
.BR kill (2).
A positive
.I pid
names a process, 0 names every process in the caller's process
group, and a negative
.I pid
names every process in process group
.RI | pid |.
Each
.I pid
must be a whole decimal integer; anything else is diagnosed and
skipped rather than being treated as 0.
.PP
A
.I signal
may be given as a number (0 up to 31) or as a name, with or without
the
.B SIG
prefix, in any letter case.
Signal 0 performs the permission and existence checks without
sending anything.
.PP
Only the first argument is examined for an option.
To name a process group with a negative
.I pid
while using the default signal, put
.B \-\-
first.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-s " signal\fR, " \-\-signal " signal"
Send
.I signal
instead of
.BR SIGTERM .
.TP
.BI \- signal
Same as
.BR \-s ,
for example
.B \-9
or
.BR \-KILL .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
With no further arguments, print every known signal name, without
the
.B SIG
prefix, on one line.
Otherwise decode each argument: a number is taken as a signal number
(or, if greater than 128, as an exit status from which 128 is
subtracted) and its name is printed; a name is translated to its
number.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options.
.PP
The names known to
.B kill
are:
.BR HUP ", " INT ", " QUIT ", " ILL ", " TRAP ", " ABRT ", " IOT ", "
.BR BUS ", " FPE ", " KILL ", " USR1 ", " SEGV ", " USR2 ", " PIPE ", "
.BR ALRM ", " TERM ", " CHLD ", " CONT ", " STOP ", " TSTP ", " TTIN ", "
.BR TTOU ", " URG ", " XCPU ", " XFSZ ", " VTALRM ", " PROF ", " WINCH ", "
.BR IO ", " POLL ", and " SYS .
Real-time signals have no name and must be given by number.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every process was signalled (or, with
.BR \-l ,
every argument was decoded).
.TP
.B 1
No
.I pid
was given, a signal or
.I pid
was invalid, or at least one
.BR kill (2)
call failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Terminate a process politely, then forcibly:
.PP
.EX
$ kill 1234
$ kill \-KILL 1234
.EE
.PP
Send SIGHUP to every process in process group 500:
.PP
.EX
$ kill \-s HUP \-\- \-500
.EE
.PP
Find which signal killed a job that exited with status 137:
.PP
.EX
$ kill \-l 137
KILL
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR kill (2),
.BR signal (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024.
The long options
.B \-\-signal
and
.B \-\-list
follow GNU conventions.
.SH BUGS
Job-control operands such as
.B %1
are not supported by this utility; use the shell's built-in
.B kill
for those.
